{
  "teamId": 147,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 New York Yankees were a team defined by inconsistency, resilience, and ultimately, heartbreak. They stumbled through April at 12-14, unable to string together the kind of dominant stretches that Yankee fans expected, yet individual moments — a Kyle Higashioka line drive here, a Gleyber Torres clutch double there — kept the season from feeling lost before it ever began. The bullpen, anchored by Chad Green and Aroldis Chapman, showed flashes of brilliance but also alarming vulnerability, foreshadowing the fragility that would haunt them all year.\n\nMay and June brought a rollercoaster of high drama, with crushing homer-allowed moments offset by stunning individual heroics. Gio Urshela's three-run blast, Rougned Odor's go-ahead shot in extra innings, Gary Sanchez's big fly, and even a rare triple play engineered by the defense gave the Bronx faithful reason to believe. But the wins were never comfortable. The Yankees limped into July at a modest pace, and Jose Altuve tormented Chad Green twice — first in May, then with a devastating three-run homer in July — serving as a recurring nightmare that underscored New York's inability to close out games cleanly.\n\nAugust was the season's defining chapter. The Yankees went a scorching 21-8, their best month of the year, powered by a newly invigorated lineup that now featured trade-deadline acquisitions Anthony Rizzo and Joey Gallo. Giancarlo Stanton emerged as a force, headlined by a two-run blast on August 12th, and the offense finally clicked into a rhythm that matched the team's pitching. They clawed their way into postseason contention through September, with Stanton's late-September grand slam punctuating a drive that earned them a Wild Card berth, even if the finish line came with an asterisk.\n\nOctober told a brief, brutal story. Facing the Boston Red Sox in the Wild Card Game at Fenway Park, Gerrit Cole — the $324 million ace and supposed ace of aces — was undone by home runs from Xander Bogaerts and Kyle Schwarber, and the Yankees never truly recovered. A lone Anthony Rizzo homer offered hope, but a base-running blunder on Giancarlo Stanton's single — Judge thrown out at the plate — and late insurance runs off the bullpen sealed the fate of a 92-win team. The Yankees' season ended in a single night, as it had in 2018 and 2020, leaving questions about whether this core could ever get past October's first hurdle.",
